+## Counterexample
+
+Choose $\kappa>0$ in $\mathrm{m^{-1}}$ and $E_0>0$ in
+$\mathrm{V\,m^{-1}}$. Set $k=\kappa e_3$, $\omega=c\kappa$, $e=E_0e_1$,
+and $b=(E_0/c)e_2$, where $e_1=(1,0,0)$, $e_2=(0,1,0)$ and
+$e_3=(0,0,1)$ are the positively oriented coordinate unit vectors. Let $E,B$
+be the real monochromatic plane-wave fields with phase $k\cdot x-\omega t$.
+
+1.1 Since $E_0>0$, $e\ne0$, and direct calculation gives
+$k\cdot e=0$, $k\times e/\omega=(E_0/c)e_2=b$, and $\omega=c|k|$.
+Therefore [F3] gives a smooth source-free Maxwell solution and [F1] makes it
+nonzero. By [F2], its period-averaged energy density is
+$\varepsilon_0E_0^2/2>0$ and its total energy over $\mathbb R^3$ is infinite.
+[given, F1, F2, F3, algebra]
+
+2.1 Every member of the Fourier-packet domain in [F4] has finite energy.
+Thus the nonzero wave of step 1.1 does not belong to that domain, refuting the
+universal statement. Componentwise formal cancellations do not make these
+all-space functionals defined on this field. [step 1.1, F4, algebra] ∎
